Step 3 (`annotate` carryover seed) decides only the all-or-nothing case: "A response with no guardrail bodies renders `_(TODO)_` rather than failing the annotation." It does not say what happens when the model response is partial or its body is unusable. Cases it leaves open:
  - the response gives one or two of the three bodies;
  - a model-written body is empty or a `TODO` placeholder;
  - a model-written body contains its own line-starting `## ` heading, which would create a duplicate heading in the seed.

  Step 2's block helper is defined only for copying an accepted source body. Step 3 says to extend that helper additively for model-written bodies, but does not name the per-heading rule. Two implementers could emit different seeds. One would write a refused seed with a duplicate or empty heading and report it as "model-written". The other would write `_(TODO)_` and list the heading as unfilled. Step 6's acceptance claim that "a seed produced by each of … `annotate` … passes when its source carries guardrails" does not cover the no-accepted-heading case. The shared helper decides this, and step 2 owns it.
